Japanese Mega Drive packaging, reproduced here as design references. Artwork and branding © SEGA. There's a useful [Japanese report of Yoichi Takahashi explaining the original packages on Sega's 2020 broadcast](https://game.watch.impress.co.jp/docs/news/1279221.html). Naoto Ohshima had asked for something new; Takahashi wanted visual impact beyond the usual game package. He commissioned Akira Watanabe, known for airbrushed car illustrations, to paint the characters. For *Spinball*, [Hayato Takebayashi identifies himself as the packaging designer](https://x.com/pantaloon_n/status/1320632944696852482). [VGDensetsu's cover archive](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/) records the more detailed illustration credits separately.

There are some good trails from there: [Takebayashi's other packaging, including *Sonic CD*, *Sega Rally* and *Virtual-On*](https://vgdensetsu.net/hayatotakebayashi/), [Watanabe's illustration archive and links to his own portfolio](https://vgdensetsu.net/akirawatanabe/), and [translated interviews with Ohshima about his design process](https://shmuplations.com/sonic/). The interviews cover the characters and games rather than the packaging.

I borrowed the yellow disc for the headline, with the first line in black and the second in cobalt. The short description of what I do sits on a black ribbon: “Platforms. Content. People. Connected by design.” A red diagonal sits behind the blue project card.

The squiggles, triangles and serrated edges were fun to work with. Some turned up around code blocks and quotations too, with the decoration kept out of the text itself.

The packaging vocabulary has had a longer life than I'd realised. Look at the Japanese design in [SEGA's *Sonic Origins Plus* poster gallery](https://origins.sonicthehedgehog.com/posters/index.html): the yellow disc, tilted black label, “THE MOST FAMOUS HEDGEHOG” tag, scattered marks and serrated edge are all there again. There's also [an interview with its cover illustrator, Mark Hughes, at *The SEGA Lounge*](https://www.thesegalounge.com/228-mark-hughes/), for another route into the later artwork.

For earlier visual comparisons, I also looked at [Sottsass’s *Casablanca* and *Carlton* drawings at the V&A](https://www.vam.ac.uk/info/collection-selection-boxes-postmodern-design) and [April Greiman’s work in *Eye on Design*](https://eyeondesign.aiga.org/april-greiman-is-still-ahead-of-the-curve/). Those are parallels I noticed in the angles, lettering and collage, rather than a documented account of what inspired the Sonic covers or the TurboGrafx ad.

## A second reference: fragments on paper

I also had a TurboGrafx-16 growing up and loved it and its personality. [This ad in *Electronic Gaming Monthly*](https://archives.tg-16.com/EGM_1989_07.htm) caught my eye too: a face and game screenshots cut into odd shapes, with bits of red and yellow overlapping them. There is a lot happening, but also a surprising amount of empty paper.

