{% marginnote %}A contemporary lead: [*Television Digest*, 2 October 1989, p. 13](https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-TV-Digest/80s/1989/Television-Digest-1989-10.pdf#page=13), names DDB Needham in New York as the agency for NEC's TurboGrafx-16 campaign, including print ads. That report is later than this spread and doesn't identify its designer.{% endmarginnote %}

For the wider story, [Christian Nutt's TurboGrafx-16 oral history](https://www.gamedeveloper.com/business/stalled-engine-the-turbografx-16-turns-25) includes NEC staff discussing the American rebranding and packaging decisions. [Jason Breininger's illustrated packaging series at *Cartridge Corner*](https://cartridgecorner.blog/2018/10/08/turbografx-16-box-artwork-part-1-they-did-the-game-series-thing-too/) looks more closely at the colour-coded boxes, spine labels and cover art. It's a collector's critique, with examples to browse.

I wanted to try something similar with images in posts. The [cut-paper collage study](/design-lab/collage/) uses a screenshot of the Mangrove Figma library, with cropped details and a black shape behind it. The whole screenshot is still there to look at.

It's now an optional figure treatment, used here and in the Johnny Castaway article.

